Order isomorphisms among power sets of the naturals, of pairs of naturals, and of the product of two power sets, induced by the Cantor pairing and the sum–product equivalence.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`numEquiv`).** The Cantor pairing and its inverse form an equivalence between pairs of naturals and naturals.

*Proof.* The two-sided inverse laws for the pairing. ∎

**Theorem (`numEquiv_apply`).** The equivalence on a pair is the pairing of its components.

*Proof.* By definition. ∎

**Definition (`setCongr`).** An equivalence of types induces an order isomorphism of power sets by direct image.

*Proof.* Direct and inverse images are mutual inverses; inclusion is preserved both ways by injectivity. ∎

**Definition (`PN_orderIso_PNN`).** Power sets of naturals are order-isomorphic to power sets of pairs via the inverse pairing.

*Proof.* Apply the set congruence to the symmetric pairing equivalence. ∎

**Definition (`PN_orderIso_prod`).** Power sets of naturals are order-isomorphic to a product of two power sets via the sum–natural equivalence.

*Proof.* Compose the set congruence of the sum equivalence with the sum–product isomorphism of sets. ∎

**Definition (`PNN_orderIso_prod`).** Power sets of pairs are order-isomorphic to a product of two power sets.

*Proof.* Transport the previous isomorphism along the pairing isomorphism. ∎
